A bow is useless without ammunition that fits it.  Each bow accepts exactly one
of the eight ammunition types and refuses all others, so a hunting bow will
never fire a quarrel and a crossbow will never fire a hunting arrow.  EVALUATE
a bow and it will tell you what it fires; EVALUATE the ammunition and it will
tell you what it is.  See ARROWS for the eight types.

Every bow has its own maximum range, measured in rooms, and this varies from
bow to bow rather than by type - some short bows outrange some long ones.  You
cannot shoot beyond that range no matter how skilled you are; the shot will be
refused rather than the arrow wasted.  EVALUATE will estimate a bow's range if
you know enough to judge it.

Long-stalk bows are drawn by strength alone.  A weak archer cannot bend a heavy
one at all and will fail to load it, and the greater the bow's reach the more
strength it demands.  An archer of ordinary build can manage the smaller long
bows, while the finest need considerable power behind them.  Crossbows and
blowpipes make no such demand - the mechanism or the breath does the work,
not your back.

Bows are not indestructible.  Every shot risks the string, and a snapped string
must be repaired before the bow will fire again.  Worse, a bow can shatter
outright as you draw it.  Both grow likelier as the bow wears, so a bow in poor
condition is a liability at exactly the moment you need it.

Ranged weapons cannot be used in a peaceful room, nor against a target in one.
Some rooms are closed to mobs, and a shot cannot pass through such a room.

Specialization in ranged weapons improves both your accuracy and the damage
your shots deal, and it is the only way to loose more than one shot in a round
with the heavier ammunition.

